-- title:
--   Tₚ is a unit in the corner ring at auxiliary level Nr
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, and let $p\neq 2$ be a prime lying in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$. Let $\bar\rho$ be a two-dimensional residual Galois representation over $k$ (a representation of $\mathrm{Aut}(\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ on a $k$-space of rank $2$ trivial on some finite level) which is absolutely irreducible, i.e. irreducible after base change to $\bar k$, and ordinary at $p$: for each valuation subring $P$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ in which $p$ is a nonunit there is a line $L$ spanned by a basis vector, stable under the decomposition group at $P$, with $\bar\rho(\sigma)v-v\in L$ for all $\sigma$ in inertia. Let $S$ be a finite set of primes with $p\in S$, and $N\neq 0$ with $p\nmid N$ and all prime divisors of $N$ in $S$. A subset $Smin\subseteq S$ containing $p$ records the ramification of $\bar\rho$: for primes $q\neq p$, $q\in Smin$ iff $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$; each $q\in Smin$, $q\neq p$, divides $N$; primes $q\neq p$ outside $Smin$ dividing $N$ satisfy $q^2\mid N$; no $q\neq p$ has $q^3\mid N$; and at each $q\in Smin$, $q\neq p$, inertia acts through matrices of characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$. An auxiliary prime $r\geq 5$ is taken with $r\notin S$, $r\nmid Np$, $p\nmid r-1$, and the level-raising obstruction $\mathrm{tr}\,\bar\rho(\mathrm{Frob}_r)^2\neq (r+1)^2$ at every valuation subring over $r$ and every Frobenius element there; $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ is assumed to carry an integral structure, in the sense that the $\mathbb C$-span of the submodule spanned by the forms with integral $q$-coefficients is everything. Further data: a ring homomorphism $\theta_0$ from the Hecke algebra $\mathrm{heckeAlgebra}\,N\,2\,S$ to $k$ whose values on $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$) give $\mathrm{charpoly}\,\bar\rho(\mathrm{Frob}_\ell)=X^2-\theta_0(T_\ell)X+\ell$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\pi_{T_0}$ from the local Hecke algebra $\mathrm{heckeLocal}\,N\,S\,\mathcal O\,\theta_0$ to $\mathcal O$; the subgroup $H_0\subseteq(\mathbb Z/Nr)^\times$ of units congruent to $1$ modulo $r$; and, on the subfamily of generators of level $Nr$ consisting of the $T_\ell$, the diamonds and only those $U_q$ with $q\mid N$, the assumption `hcomm` that the corresponding operators on $H^1=\mathrm{Hom}(\Gamma_{H_0}(Nr),\mathcal O)$ commute pairwise. A function $cU$ into $\mathcal O$ and a function $\bar\theta$ into $k$ on this subfamily satisfy $\bar\theta(T_\ell)=\theta_0(T_\ell)$, $\bar\theta(U_q)=$ residue of $cU(U_q)$ for $q\mid N$, $\bar\theta(\langle d\rangle)=1$, together with $cU(U_q)\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$ for every prime $q\mid N$ with $q\neq p$ and $q^2\mid N$. Finally let $Sp$ be an idempotent splitting of the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathcal O}H^1$ generated by the subfamily operators, $i$ an index of that splitting, $\pi_k$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map from the corner ring $e_i(\cdot)e_i$ to $k$ carrying the image of the operator attached to each subfamily generator $g$ to $\bar\theta(g)$, and assume every $\sigma\in\Gamma_0(Nr)$ acts as the identity through $\mathrm{diamondRaw}$ on the corner submodule $e_iH^1$. The conclusion is that there exists an element $t_p$ of the corner ring which is a unit and which, acting on the corner submodule $e_iH^1$, coincides both with the Hecke operator $\mathrm{heckeT}\,(Nr)\,H_0\,p\,\mathcal O$ and with its lower companion $\mathrm{heckeTlower}\,(Nr)\,H_0\,p\,\mathcal O$.
--
--   Since $p$ lies in $S$, the operator $T_p$ is not among the chosen generators of the subfamily Hecke algebra, so its realisation inside the corner ring is genuine content; ordinarity of $\bar\rho$ at $p$ is what makes the realising element a unit, and the simultaneous identification with the lower companion operator records the compatibility used when comparing the two degeneracy maps at the auxiliary prime $r$. It feeds the construction of the $H^1$ corner data and its pairing with the residual eigensystem at level $Nr$ in the Taylor–Wiles patching argument.
